Usages:
  convention: .goga/usages/conventions.md
  requests: .goga/usages/cooks/requests.md
  httpx: .goga/usages/cooks/httpx.md

Annotations: |
  Use `convention` for code style, Google-style docstrings (Args/Returns/Raises),
  relative imports, the mirrored test structure, and Python 3.10+ typing
  (typing.Optional per UP045). pydantic is NOT used in this cell.

  The cell is the HTTP roundtrip core: clients (`Requests`, `Session`), response
  wrappers (`BaseResponse`/`Response`/`AsyncResponse`), and the polling routines
  (`poll`, `apoll`).

  Two engines live side by side: the sync path uses `requests`; the async path uses
  `httpx`. Keep behavior in parity across them (status checks, headers, json, reload).

  TIMEOUT OVERLOAD (document in docstrings): the constructor timeout is the
  NETWORK timeout (connect/read, set once, maps to the `requests` timeout / an
  `httpx` Timeout); the method timeout is the POLLING window. Same name, different
  meaning by position.

  The kwargs: dict[str, str] parameter and the dict[str, str] return of
  json() are DSL approximations of heterogeneous runtime values forwarded verbatim
  to the underlying engine — do not coerce or validate them.

  reload/areload are IN-PLACE: replace the wrapped underlying on the same
  object so existing references observe the update.

---

"BaseResponse(owner: Requests | Session, method: str, path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str])":
  location: responses.py
  annotations: |
    Common ancestor of the sync and async response wrappers. Stores the request
    recipe (method, path, kwargs) and a back-reference to the owning client, and
    defines the unified attribute-proxy contract used by both engines.

    `owner`: the client that produced the response; used to re-execute the recipe.
    `method`: HTTP verb of the original request.
    `path`: request path joined onto owner.base_url.
    `kwargs`: forwarded engine keyword arguments, stored verbatim for reload.

    Requirements:
    - Store the recipe and `owner` so subclasses can re-execute the request.
    - Expose the underlying response through the explicit properties below
      (unified names), mapping engine-specific names (`requests` ok / `httpx`
      is_success; `requests` reason / `httpx` reason_phrase).

    Constraints:
    - Do not use __getattr__ for proxying — declare explicit properties only.
    - Do not re-execute requests at this level; subclasses own reload/areload.
  properties:
    "status_code -> int": |
      HTTP status code of the underlying response.
    "ok -> bool": |
      True for a success status (maps to `requests` ok / `httpx` is_success).
    "text -> str": |
      Decoded body of the underlying response.
    "content -> bytes": |
      Raw body bytes of the underlying response.
    "headers -> dict[str, str]": |
      Response headers (case-insensitive in the underlying engine).
    "url -> str": |
      Final URL after redirects.
    "encoding -> str | None": |
      Body encoding, or None when absent.
  methods:
    "json() -> body:dict[str, str]": |
      Parsed JSON body of the underlying response.

      Returns:
      Parsed JSON; dict[str, str] is a DSL approximation of a heterogeneous
      runtime structure.
    "raise_for_status()": |
      Delegate to the underlying raise_for_status (raises the engine HTTP error
      on 4xx/5xx).

"BaseResponse::Response(owner: Requests | Session, method: str, path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str])":
  location: responses.py
  annotations: |
    Sync wrapper over a `requests` `Response`. Inherits the proxy contract from
    `BaseResponse`; adds in-place reload.

    Requirements:
    - The wrapped underlying is a `requests` `Response`.

    Use `requests` for the reload execution (urljoin path normalization, network
    timeout, recipe replay via the `requests` generic dispatcher).
  methods:
    "reload()": |
      Re-execute the original request and replace the wrapped `requests` `Response`
      in place, preserving object identity.

      Algorithm:
      1. Re-execute the stored recipe through owner (network timeout =
         owner.timeout, path joined with normalization), producing a new
         underlying response.
      2. Replace the wrapped underlying on this same object.

      Requirements:
      - Preserve object identity — all existing references observe the update.
      - Network timeout comes from owner.timeout.

      Constraints:
      - Do not construct a new `Response`; mutate the existing one.

"BaseResponse::AsyncResponse(owner: Requests | Session, method: str, path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str])":
  location: responses.py
  annotations: |
    Async wrapper over an `httpx` `Response`. Inherits the proxy contract from
    `BaseResponse`; adds in-place areload.

    Requirements:
    - The wrapped underlying is an `httpx` `Response`.

    Use `httpx` for the reload execution (replay against the same AsyncClient
    that produced the original response; httpx.Timeout; path-join parity).
  methods:
    "areload()": |
      Re-await the original request and replace the wrapped `httpx` `Response`
      in place, preserving object identity.

      Algorithm:
      1. Re-await the stored recipe through owner against the same AsyncClient
         that produced the original response.
      2. Replace the wrapped underlying on this same object.

      Requirements:
      - Preserve object identity — all existing references observe the update.
      - Reuse the owner's AsyncClient instance (it owns the connection pool).

      Constraints:
      - Do not construct a new `AsyncResponse`; mutate the existing one.

"poll(owner: Requests | Session, method: str, path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: Response":
  location: polling.py
  annotations: |
    Synchronous polling loop: execute a request, raise_for_status, and on a bad
    status retry after `delay` until success or until the `timeout` window elapses.

    `owner`: the client that executes the request.
    `method`: HTTP verb.
    `path`: request path joined onto owner.base_url.
    `kwargs`: forwarded engine keyword arguments.
    `timeout`: polling window in seconds (None disables polling).
    `delay`: seconds to wait between attempts (default 1.0; ignored when
    `timeout` is None).
    `response`: the successful `Response`, or the last (bad-status) `Response`
    if the window elapses without a success status.

    Algorithm:
    1. If `timeout` is None, execute the request once through `owner` and return
       the `Response` without calling raise_for_status (plain `requests`
       behavior).
    2. Otherwise set a deadline measured from the call start (`timeout`).
    3. Execute the request to obtain a `Response`.
    4. Loop: call Response.raise_for_status; on success (2xx) return the
       `Response`.
    5. On an engine HTTP error: if the deadline has elapsed, RETURN the last
       (bad-status) `Response` (no exception); otherwise sleep `delay`, call
       reload on the same `Response`, and repeat.

    Requirements:
    - Measure the deadline from the start of the call, not per attempt.
    - On window expiry, return the last `Response` (do not raise) so the caller
      can inspect its status or call reload to retry.

    Constraints:
    - Retry only on a bad-status HTTP error; do not retry transport-level
      failures (connection/timeout/TLS) — let them propagate immediately.
    - Do not raise on window expiry; return the last response.

    Use `requests` for execution semantics.

"apoll(owner: Requests | Session, method: str, path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: AsyncResponse":
  location: polling.py
  annotations: |
    Asynchronous polling loop — the async counterpart of `poll`.

    `owner`: the client that executes the request (owns the AsyncClient).
    `method`: HTTP verb.
    `path`: request path joined onto owner.base_url.
    `kwargs`: forwarded engine keyword arguments.
    `timeout`: polling window in seconds (None disables polling).
    `delay`: seconds to await between attempts (default 1.0; ignored when
    `timeout` is None).
    `response`: the successful `AsyncResponse`, or the last (bad-status)
    `AsyncResponse` if the window elapses without a success status.

    Algorithm:
    1. If `timeout` is None, await the request once through `owner` and return
       the `AsyncResponse` without calling raise_for_status.
    2. Otherwise set a deadline measured from the call start (`timeout`).
    3. Await the request to obtain an `AsyncResponse`.
    4. Loop: call AsyncResponse.raise_for_status; on success (2xx) return the
       `AsyncResponse`.
    5. On an engine HTTP error: if the deadline has elapsed, RETURN the last
       (bad-status) `AsyncResponse` (no exception); otherwise await `delay`
       (async sleep), call areload on the same `AsyncResponse`, and repeat.

    Requirements:
    - Measure the deadline from the start of the call, not per attempt.
    - On window expiry, return the last `AsyncResponse` (do not raise) so the
      caller can inspect its status or call areload to retry.

    Constraints:
    - Retry only on a bad-status HTTP error; do not retry transport-level
      failures — let them propagate immediately.
    - Do not raise on window expiry; return the last response.

    Use `httpx` for execution semantics.

"Requests(base_url: str, timeout: float | None)":
  location: clients.py
  annotations: |
    HTTP client: sync verbs built on `requests` (a new connection per call) and
    async a* verbs built on a lazily-created, long-lived `httpx` AsyncClient
    whose connection pool is reused across a* calls and areload.

    `base_url`: origin prefixed to every request path.
    `timeout`: NETWORK timeout (connect/read), set once here; maps to the
    `requests` timeout and an `httpx` Timeout. It is NOT the polling window.

    Requirements:
    - Sync verbs open a fresh connection per call (module-level `requests`
      behavior).
    - Async verbs reuse a single long-lived AsyncClient, created lazily on the
      first a* call and held for the life of the client.
    - Support async with: __aenter__ returns self, __aexit__ awaits aclose;
      release the AsyncClient via aclose (or async with) when done.

    Use `requests` for sync execution and `httpx` for async execution.
  properties:
    "base_url -> str": |
      Configured request origin.
    "timeout -> float | None": |
      Configured network timeout.
  methods:
    "get(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: Response": |
      Sync GET request against base_url.

      `path`: request path, normalized and joined onto base_url.
      `kwargs`: forwarded verbatim to the `requests` call.
      `timeout`: POLLING window (None = single request, no auto raise_for_status).
      `delay`: seconds between polling attempts (default 1.0).
      `response`: the `Response`.

      Algorithm:
      1. If `timeout` is None, execute one GET through the sync engine and return
         the `Response` without auto raise_for_status.
      2. Otherwise delegate to `poll` with the recipe and the polling window.

      Requirements:
      - Network timeout is the constructor `timeout`, not the method `timeout`.
      - Forward `kwargs` verbatim.

      Use `requests` for execution.
    "post(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: Response": |
      Same as get, issuing a POST. Use `requests`.
    "put(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: Response": |
      Same as get, issuing a PUT. Use `requests`.
    "delete(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: Response": |
      Same as get, issuing a DELETE. Use `requests`.
    "patch(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: Response": |
      Same as get, issuing a PATCH. Use `requests`.
    "head(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: Response": |
      Same as get, issuing a HEAD. Use `requests`.
    "options(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: Response": |
      Same as get, issuing an OPTIONS. Use `requests`.
    "aget(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: AsyncResponse": |
      Async GET request against base_url via the long-lived `httpx` AsyncClient.

      `path`: request path, normalized and joined onto base_url.
      `kwargs`: forwarded verbatim to the `httpx` call.
      `timeout`: POLLING window (None = single request, no auto raise_for_status).
      `delay`: seconds between polling attempts (default 1.0).
      `response`: the `AsyncResponse`.

      Algorithm:
      1. If `timeout` is None, execute one GET through the long-lived async client
         and return the `AsyncResponse` without auto raise_for_status.
      2. Otherwise delegate to `apoll` with the recipe and the polling window.

      Requirements:
      - Reuse the long-lived AsyncClient (created lazily on the first a* call).
      - Network timeout is the constructor `timeout`.

      Use `httpx` for execution.
    "apost(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: AsyncResponse": |
      Same as aget, issuing a POST. Use `httpx`.
    "aput(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: AsyncResponse": |
      Same as aget, issuing a PUT. Use `httpx`.
    "adelete(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: AsyncResponse": |
      Same as aget, issuing a DELETE. Use `httpx`.
    "apatch(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: AsyncResponse": |
      Same as aget, issuing a PATCH. Use `httpx`.
    "ahead(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: AsyncResponse": |
      Same as aget, issuing a HEAD. Use `httpx`.
    "aoptions(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: AsyncResponse": |
      Same as aget, issuing an OPTIONS. Use `httpx`.
    "aclose()": |
      Close the long-lived `httpx` AsyncClient and release its resources.

      Requirements:
      - Idempotent; safe to call after closing.
      - No-op when no a* call has created the client yet (lazy creation).
      - Also called by __aexit__ when the client is used as async with.

"Session(base_url: str, timeout: float | None)":
  location: clients.py
  annotations: |
    HTTP client with a persistent sync connection (`requests` `Session`) and a
    long-lived async connection (`httpx` AsyncClient).

    `base_url`: origin prefixed to every request path.
    `timeout`: NETWORK timeout (connect/read), set once here; maps to the
    `requests` session timeout and an `httpx` Timeout.

    Requirements:
    - Reuse one `requests` `Session` across sync calls (persistent pool/cookies).
    - Hold one long-lived AsyncClient for async calls; expose aclose to
      release it.
    - Support async with: __aenter__ returns self, __aexit__ awaits aclose.

    Use `requests` for sync execution and `httpx` for async execution.
  properties:
    "base_url -> str": |
      Configured request origin.
    "timeout -> float | None": |
      Configured network timeout.
  methods:
    "get(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: Response": |
      Sync GET through the persistent `requests` `Session`. Same Algorithm and
      Requirements as Requests.get (single request when `timeout` is None, else
      delegate to `poll`), executed against the held `requests` `Session`.

      Use `requests` for execution.
    "post(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: Response": |
      Same as get, issuing a POST through the `requests` `Session`. Use `requests`.
    "put(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: Response": |
      Same as get, issuing a PUT. Use `requests`.
    "delete(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: Response": |
      Same as get, issuing a DELETE. Use `requests`.
    "patch(path: str, kwargs: dict[str, str], timeout: float | None, delay: float) -> response: Response": |
